<h1 id="pgpbp-title" style="color:#0d47a1;font-size:1.5em;font-weight:700;border-bottom:2px solid #90caf9;padding-bottom:0.25em;margin-top:0"><code>pgpbp</code> — pgbattest paragraph play-by-play (Door A / B / C)</h1>

**Type:** keyword autopsy · **Not always-on** — mention **`pgpbp`** or `@` this rule.  
**Owns:** for a **recorded** scoreboard run, find every paragraph that **caused** a Door A, B, or C fail, reconstruct how it was generated/processed, and show a **cause-centered** play-by-play table (freshman-plain) with the **relevant** produce/verify prompt excerpts and Injection Gate results.  
**Does not own:** starting a new `pgbattest` campaign; shipping product fixes (`aciub` / implement is separate).

May **call other rules as tools** when they help the same end table: **`serlog`** (gitignored `logs/llm_traffic`), **`pgjudge`** (flake vs real A/B signal), **`evalay`** (optional lay gloss). The **deliverable is still the pgpbp tables** — do not stop at a short `pgjudge` 1–2–3 reply.

## Document outline

1. [When this applies](#when) — trigger + parse.
2. [Resolve the run](#run) — scoreboard §6.xx → log + Door C JSON.
3. [Resolve scopes](#scopes) — explicit list vs failing scopes.
4. [What each door failure means](#doors) — A / B / C and which paragraphs to autopsy.
5. [Where to dig](#artifacts) — scoreboard, harness, Door C JSON, LLM traces.
6. [Per-paragraph procedure](#procedure) — produce → verify → gate → punch → judges.
7. [Response shape](#shape) — attempt-matrix tables (mandatory).
8. [Examples](#examples) — copy-paste prompts.
9. [Related rules](#related).

